WEBVTT

00:00:06.924 --> 00:00:08.926
[radio static]

00:00:12.888 --> 00:00:13.722
[beep]

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:15.807
[indistinct radio chatter]

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:22.814
[David Attenborough] <i>Just 50 years ago,</i>

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.276
<i>we finally ventured to the Moon.</i>

00:00:40.332 --> 00:00:42.167
<i>For the very first time,</i>

00:00:42.918 --> 00:00:45.837
<i>we looked back at our own planet.</i>

00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:58.600
<i>Since then, the human population</i>
<i>has more than doubled.</i>

00:01:04.147 --> 00:01:08.610
<i>This series will celebrate</i>
<i>the natural wonders that remain</i>

00:01:09.528 --> 00:01:12.573
<i>and reveal what we must preserve</i>

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:16.868
<i>to ensure people and nature thrive.</i>

00:01:44.563 --> 00:01:45.480
<i>Dolphins...</i>

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.025
<i>panicking.</i>

00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:56.867
<i>They're being hunted</i>
<i>by false killer whales.</i>

00:01:58.368 --> 00:02:00.621
[birds cawing]

00:02:13.258 --> 00:02:16.219
<i>Dolphins are faster in short bursts.</i>

00:02:25.562 --> 00:02:27.731
<i>But their hunters have stamina.</i>

00:02:37.574 --> 00:02:39.701
<i>Just as the killer whales close in,</i>

00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:45.749
<i>the dolphins make a sudden turn</i>
<i>to evade their predators.</i>

00:02:59.638 --> 00:03:04.184
<i>These extraordinary events</i>
<i>are taking place far from land,</i>

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:06.144
<i>where few of us ever venture.</i>

00:03:10.273 --> 00:03:14.319
<i>This is the ocean</i>
<i>beyond the boundary of any country...</i>

00:03:15.112 --> 00:03:19.449
<i>largely ungoverned, wild, and lawless.</i>

00:03:34.256 --> 00:03:38.385
<i>Nothing symbolizes</i>
<i>our relationship with the high seas</i>

00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:41.263
<i>better than the blue whale.</i>

00:03:48.562 --> 00:03:52.441
<i>Weighing 200 tons and 30 meters long,</i>

00:03:52.524 --> 00:03:56.278
<i>these are the largest animals</i>
<i>ever to have existed.</i>

00:03:57.529 --> 00:04:01.575
<i>Yet much of their lives</i>
<i>is still a mystery to us.</i>

00:04:07.581 --> 00:04:10.542
<i>Blue whales live far out to sea,</i>

00:04:12.335 --> 00:04:16.173
<i>roaming every ocean</i>
<i>from the tropics to the poles.</i>

00:04:22.137 --> 00:04:24.473
<i>We have only recently discovered</i>

00:04:24.556 --> 00:04:29.186
<i>a few special places</i>
<i>where they come close to shore.</i>

00:04:35.776 --> 00:04:37.527
<i>The Gulf of California,</i>

00:04:40.906 --> 00:04:42.991
<i>on Mexico's Pacific coast.</i>

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.177
<i>This is a sanctuary for blue whales.</i>

00:05:15.023 --> 00:05:16.274
[whale huffing]

00:05:17.818 --> 00:05:21.029
<i>Meet the world's biggest newborn...</i>

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:25.826
<i>a baby blue.</i>

00:05:27.369 --> 00:05:28.787
<i>Just a month old,</i>

00:05:29.621 --> 00:05:34.334
<i>she's already eight meters long</i>
<i>and weighs six tons.</i>

00:05:37.128 --> 00:05:40.757
<i>Her mother shelters her</i>
<i>with a protective fin.</i>

00:05:45.512 --> 00:05:50.684
<i>They caress one another,</i>
<i>cherishing their close bond.</i>

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.023
<i>These are the most intimate images</i>
<i>ever captured</i>

00:05:57.107 --> 00:06:00.735
<i>of a blue whale mother and her calf.</i>

00:06:19.087 --> 00:06:23.925
<i>This female will give birth</i>
<i>only once every two or three years,</i>

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:27.846
<i>so each baby blue is extremely precious.</i>

00:06:32.976 --> 00:06:36.271
<i>The calf will grow three tons a month.</i>

00:06:48.116 --> 00:06:52.537
<i>Once, there were</i>
<i>more than</i> <i>300,000 blue whales,</i>

00:06:53.872 --> 00:06:57.000
<i>but last century,</i>
<i>they were hunted relentlessly,</i>

00:06:57.792 --> 00:07:00.295
<i>and only a few thousand survived.</i>

00:07:09.888 --> 00:07:11.890
<i>Blue whales are now protected,</i>

00:07:13.058 --> 00:07:15.268
<i>and their numbers are slowly increasing.</i>

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.069
<i>Soon, this calf will leave</i>
<i>these sheltered waters</i>

00:07:24.986 --> 00:07:26.863
<i>to roam the high seas.</i>

00:07:37.624 --> 00:07:42.462
<i>We once thought that the high seas</i>
<i>were simply too vast for us to damage,</i>

00:07:46.967 --> 00:07:52.055
<i>but we now know that her ocean home</i>
<i>is under threat as never before.</i>

00:08:02.774 --> 00:08:07.946
<i>Out here in the big blue,</i>
<i>there remain a few pristine places</i>

00:08:08.029 --> 00:08:12.158
<i>to remind us</i>
<i>how rich the oceans can still be.</i>

00:08:23.878 --> 00:08:25.255
<i>Spinner dolphins.</i>

00:08:28.425 --> 00:08:32.429
<i>In the Pacific, off the coast </i>
<i>of Costa Rica, Central America,</i>

00:08:32.512 --> 00:08:34.848
<i>they are starting to gather</i>
<i>in great numbers.</i>

00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:40.687
<i>Spinners live in pods,</i>

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:44.983
<i>groups of individuals</i>
<i>linked by complex social relationships.</i>

00:08:47.986 --> 00:08:49.571
[dolphins clicking]

00:08:56.077 --> 00:08:59.706
<i>The dolphins constantly touch</i>
<i>and talk with one another,</i>

00:08:59.789 --> 00:09:03.043
<i>using a language of clicks and whistles.</i>

00:09:03.126 --> 00:09:05.545
[dolphins clicking and chittering]

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.969
<i>They are shadowed by yellowfin tuna.</i>

00:09:16.264 --> 00:09:19.476
<i>The tuna rely on the dolphins</i>
<i>to find food.</i>

00:09:21.978 --> 00:09:24.606
<i>Everyone is searching for the same prey...</i>

00:09:27.442 --> 00:09:28.401
<i>lantern fish...</i>

00:09:29.569 --> 00:09:31.821
<i>the most numerous fish on the planet.</i>

00:09:38.703 --> 00:09:40.455
<i>No longer than your finger,</i>

00:09:40.538 --> 00:09:43.917
<i>lantern fish spend most of their time</i>
<i>hiding in the deep.</i>

00:09:45.335 --> 00:09:49.798
<i>So, despite their astounding abundance,</i>
<i>we don't fish for them.</i>

00:09:53.593 --> 00:09:56.679
<i>They are one of the few fish</i>
<i>that still thrives.</i>

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:06.022
<i>As they close in on their prey,</i>
<i>the dolphin pod gets bigger and bigger.</i>

00:10:11.194 --> 00:10:14.364
<i>This pod could be 10,000 strong.</i>

00:10:25.792 --> 00:10:29.754
<i>For every one at the surface, </i>
<i>20 swim below.</i>

00:10:47.355 --> 00:10:49.023
[dolphins clicking]

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:58.825
<i>Using echolocation, a type of sonar,</i>

00:10:59.284 --> 00:11:02.370
<i>the dolphins detect lantern fish</i>
<i>in the depths</i>

00:11:02.871 --> 00:11:04.664
<i>and drive them to the surface.</i>

00:11:20.722 --> 00:11:23.141
<i>They patrol the underside of the shoal</i>

00:11:23.224 --> 00:11:26.186
<i>to stop their prey</i>
<i>escaping back to the deep.</i>

00:11:28.271 --> 00:11:29.689
[clicking and chittering]

00:11:43.119 --> 00:11:45.079
<i>Once the dolphins have had their fill,</i>

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:48.374
<i>there's still plenty more</i>
<i>for other predators.</i>

00:12:02.805 --> 00:12:05.266
<i>Mobula rays swoop in.</i>

00:12:16.611 --> 00:12:20.698
<i>To maximize their catch,</i>
<i>the rays attack in formation.</i>

00:12:44.722 --> 00:12:48.101
<i>A wall of mouths scooping up their prey.</i>

00:13:00.488 --> 00:13:06.703
<i>Because we don't fish for them,</i>
<i>lantern fish and their predators thrive.</i>

00:13:12.417 --> 00:13:17.088
<i>All these hunters have barely made a dent</i>
<i>in the great shoal.</i>

00:13:20.842 --> 00:13:26.514
<i>Most life in the open ocean</i>
<i>is concentrated in a thin surface layer,</i>

00:13:26.597 --> 00:13:29.684
<i>where the power of the sun</i>
<i>has its greatest effect.</i>

00:13:33.646 --> 00:13:38.484
<i>These sunlit shallows</i>
<i>are fertilized in a surprising way.</i>

00:13:41.738 --> 00:13:45.783
<i>As the dolphins produce waste,</i>
<i>they play a vital role,</i>

00:13:45.867 --> 00:13:49.704
<i>recycling nutrients from the deep</i>
<i>up to the surface.</i>

00:13:49.787 --> 00:13:51.497
[dolphins chittering]

00:13:54.542 --> 00:13:56.586
<i>This marine manure</i>

00:13:56.669 --> 00:14:02.175
<i>helps to fuel the basis</i>
<i>of all life in the open ocean.</i>

00:14:11.851 --> 00:14:13.353
<i>Phytoplankton...</i>

00:14:14.812 --> 00:14:17.482
<i>microscopic drifting plants.</i>

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:23.404
<i>They combine nutrients in seawater</i>
<i>with energy from the sun</i>

00:14:23.488 --> 00:14:27.867
<i>to create the very foundation</i>
<i>of every food chain out here.</i>

00:14:32.622 --> 00:14:36.876
<i>So all animals, from the smallest fish</i>
<i>to the largest whale,</i>

00:14:37.418 --> 00:14:41.381
<i>ultimately depend </i>
<i>on these tiny plants for food.</i>

00:14:44.967 --> 00:14:49.639
<i>And in turn, the phytoplankton</i>
<i>benefits from the nutrients</i>

00:14:49.722 --> 00:14:51.599
<i>produced by these animals.</i>

00:14:56.646 --> 00:15:03.111
<i>Crucially, phytoplankton also produce</i>
<i>half the oxygen in the air we breathe.</i>

00:15:05.613 --> 00:15:07.615
<i>So, no matter where you live,</i>

00:15:07.698 --> 00:15:10.493
<i>you can thank</i>
<i>these exquisite little plants</i>

00:15:10.576 --> 00:15:12.495
<i>for every other breath you take.</i>

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:22.797
<i>In ideal conditions,</i>
<i>they can bloom to such an extent</i>

00:15:24.549 --> 00:15:28.553
<i>that great swathes of the ocean</i>
<i>turn green.</i>

00:15:45.236 --> 00:15:51.534
<i>Surprisingly, phytoplankton</i>
<i>also play a vital role in cloud formation.</i>

00:15:52.034 --> 00:15:54.036
[thunder rumbling]

00:16:01.085 --> 00:16:03.754
<i>Moisture evaporating from the ocean</i>

00:16:03.838 --> 00:16:07.967
<i>condenses around</i>
<i>tiny particles created by the plankton.</i>

00:16:13.389 --> 00:16:17.894
<i>These droplets combine</i>
<i>to grow into colossal clouds.</i>

00:16:24.859 --> 00:16:28.654
<i>They can rise 20 kilometers</i>
<i>into the atmosphere.</i>

00:16:48.508 --> 00:16:53.930
<i>These oceanic clouds</i>
<i>reflect the sun's energy back into space,</i>

00:16:54.555 --> 00:16:57.767
<i>helping to protect the Earth</i>
<i>from rising temperatures.</i>

00:17:06.317 --> 00:17:10.196
<i>Not only do the oceans</i>
<i>produce half the oxygen we breathe,</i>

00:17:10.279 --> 00:17:13.199
<i>they also drive the weather and climate,</i>

00:17:14.242 --> 00:17:18.120
<i>transporting life-giving fresh water</i>
<i>around the world.</i>

00:17:18.996 --> 00:17:20.998
[thunder rumbling]

00:17:28.297 --> 00:17:33.219
<i>The high seas</i>
<i>are the life-support system of our planet.</i>

00:17:40.142 --> 00:17:44.105
<i>They have such power</i>
<i>simply because they're so vast.</i>

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:52.697
<i>The oceans cover</i> <i>two-thirds of the globe,</i>
<i>and that's just the surface.</i>

00:17:57.201 --> 00:17:59.245
<i>They're also immensely deep.</i>

00:18:04.458 --> 00:18:08.296
<i>We can only journey</i>
<i>to this unexplored space</i>

00:18:08.379 --> 00:18:12.466
<i>in special submarines</i>
<i>that can withstand the crushing pressure.</i>

00:18:19.807 --> 00:18:24.020
<i>The average depth of the oceans</i>
<i>is nearly four kilometers,</i>

00:18:25.313 --> 00:18:29.191
<i>and the deepest point more than ten.</i>

00:18:31.986 --> 00:18:37.325
<i>The deep makes up 95 percent</i>
<i>of all the space available for life.</i>

00:18:45.541 --> 00:18:50.963
<i>This alien world</i>
<i>is home to weird and mysterious creatures.</i>

00:18:56.344 --> 00:18:59.472
<i>Few are stranger than this one.</i>

00:19:13.027 --> 00:19:18.699
<i>For years, we only knew they existed</i>
<i>from the occasional one washed ashore.</i>

00:19:24.872 --> 00:19:27.625
<i>They have hardly ever been seen alive.</i>

00:19:35.633 --> 00:19:37.009
<i>An oarfish.</i>

00:19:39.887 --> 00:19:41.263
<i>Ten meters long.</i>

00:19:53.234 --> 00:19:54.485
<i>Hanging vertically</i>

00:19:54.568 --> 00:19:57.530
<i>and using rhythmic waves</i>
<i>along their dorsal fin,</i>

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:02.576
<i>oarfish commute easily</i>
<i>between the surface and the depths.</i>

00:20:09.208 --> 00:20:14.004
<i>Below 200 meters, there's not enough light</i>
<i>for plants to grow,</i>

00:20:14.088 --> 00:20:18.968
<i>so deep-sea animals depend on</i>
<i>food sinking down from the surface...</i>

00:20:20.177 --> 00:20:21.679
<i>marine snow.</i>

00:20:25.266 --> 00:20:29.979
<i>A crown jellyfish</i>
<i>drifts with tentacles outstretched</i>

00:20:30.062 --> 00:20:31.605
<i>to ensnare pray.</i>

00:20:35.234 --> 00:20:39.905
<i>They live in every ocean,</i>
<i>to depths of seven kilometers.</i>

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:50.958
<i>The deep is so vast that these could be</i>
<i>the most abundant of all jellyfish.</i>

00:20:55.838 --> 00:20:58.632
<i>We used to think</i>
<i>the deep supported little life,</i>

00:20:59.759 --> 00:21:01.343
<i>but scientists now believe</i>

00:21:01.427 --> 00:21:05.681
<i>there are ten times more animals</i>
<i>living here than previously thought.</i>

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:11.437
<i>So, there must be many</i>
<i>yet to be discovered.</i>

00:21:27.119 --> 00:21:33.250
<i>This deep-water crustacean, Cystisoma,</i>
<i>is as clear as glass.</i>

00:21:37.546 --> 00:21:39.799
<i>It hides in plain sight.</i>

00:21:43.803 --> 00:21:45.262
<i>In this twilight zone,</i>

00:21:45.763 --> 00:21:51.602
<i>invisibility can be the difference</i>
<i>between finding a meal and being one.</i>

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:59.902
<i>Its huge eyes strain to see in the gloom.</i>

00:22:09.912 --> 00:22:13.707
<i>No light penetrates</i>
<i>below a thousand meters.</i>

00:22:18.295 --> 00:22:21.841
<i>In this darkness,</i>
<i>animals create their own light...</i>

00:22:24.051 --> 00:22:25.678
<i>bioluminescence.</i>

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:36.480
<i>A glowing lure entices victims</i>
<i>into this dragonfish's terrifying teeth.</i>

00:22:42.278 --> 00:22:47.366
<i>On most expeditions down here,</i>
<i>we are surprised by something new.</i>

00:22:54.874 --> 00:22:57.251
<i>A deep-sea anglerfish.</i>

00:23:00.671 --> 00:23:03.465
<i>Her extraordinary array of sensors</i>

00:23:03.549 --> 00:23:08.846
<i>will detect even the faintest movement</i>
<i>by prey tempted to her lure.</i>

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:20.900
<i>Here, in the deep midwater,</i>
<i>predators play a patient game.</i>

00:23:38.125 --> 00:23:40.502
<i>Eventually, we reach the bottom,</i>

00:23:42.379 --> 00:23:44.673
<i>nearly a thousand meters down.</i>

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:53.974
<i>The deep-sea plains</i>
<i>cover more than half the Earth's surface,</i>

00:23:56.101 --> 00:23:59.980
<i>and yet we know more</i>
<i>about the surface of the Moon.</i>

00:24:06.737 --> 00:24:10.449
<i>Chimaera,</i>
<i>an ancient relative of the shark,</i>

00:24:10.532 --> 00:24:11.992
<i>up to two meters long.</i>

00:24:19.249 --> 00:24:24.463
<i>Few species of this size</i>
<i>can make a living on the barren seafloor.</i>

00:24:29.969 --> 00:24:32.262
<i>Moving slowly to conserve energy,</i>

00:24:32.805 --> 00:24:36.517
<i>they use special electrical sensors</i>
<i>around their mouths</i>

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:40.187
<i>to search for scarce prey</i>
<i>buried in the sediment.</i>

00:24:49.780 --> 00:24:55.369
<i>Rocky outcrops rising from the seafloor</i>
<i>can be oases in this desert.</i>

00:24:59.289 --> 00:25:02.876
<i>This ragged-tooth shark</i>
<i>has come here for good reason.</i>

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:08.966
<i>Five hundred meters down</i>
<i>off the coast of Florida,</i>

00:25:09.466 --> 00:25:11.885
<i>a rare hot spot of life.</i>

00:25:19.184 --> 00:25:22.521
<i>Lophelia, deep-sea coral reefs.</i>

00:25:39.455 --> 00:25:41.165
<i>We used to think that corals</i>

00:25:41.248 --> 00:25:43.834
<i>were only found</i>
<i>in the warm, sunny shallows.</i>

00:25:47.004 --> 00:25:52.551
<i>But, astonishingly, deep-sea reefs</i>
<i>cover a greater area of the seafloor</i>

00:25:52.634 --> 00:25:54.678
<i>than their shallow-water relatives.</i>

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:05.105
<i>These underwater forests</i>

00:26:05.773 --> 00:26:10.444
<i>provide shelter and food</i>
<i>for a rich community of marine life.</i>

00:26:15.657 --> 00:26:19.495
<i>They're also vital nurseries</i>
<i>for many deep-sea creatures.</i>

00:26:29.922 --> 00:26:33.926
<i>The corals are made up</i>
<i>of many anemone-like individuals,</i>

00:26:34.009 --> 00:26:39.181
<i>polyps that live in colonies</i>
<i>connected by a hard skeleton.</i>

00:26:47.147 --> 00:26:50.692
<i>The polyps</i>
<i>have stinging cells in their tentacles</i>

00:26:50.776 --> 00:26:54.613
<i>to snare passing prey,</i>
<i>their only source of food.</i>

00:27:00.327 --> 00:27:03.372
<i>But they don't always</i>
<i>get to keep their catch.</i>

00:27:06.708 --> 00:27:10.170
<i>A bristle worm has set up home</i>
<i>within the coral.</i>

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:20.264
<i>It checks the polyps</i>
<i>for a freshly caught morsel...</i>

00:27:22.558 --> 00:27:24.977
<i>and steals it from the coral.</i>

00:27:27.855 --> 00:27:31.358
<i>But this theft</i>
<i>is actually a protection racket.</i>

00:27:37.865 --> 00:27:41.034
<i>Urchins attack and eat the corals.</i>

00:27:44.121 --> 00:27:45.914
<i>Time for payback.</i>

00:27:47.791 --> 00:27:52.296
<i>The urchin may have protective spines,</i>
<i>but the worm has a shocking weapon,</i>

00:27:54.173 --> 00:27:55.507
<i>and goes on the attack.</i>

00:28:18.238 --> 00:28:20.407
<i>The urchin has been seen off,</i>

00:28:22.201 --> 00:28:25.078
<i>and the worm has saved its home.</i>

00:28:27.164 --> 00:28:29.166
<i>Like most deep-sea animals,</i>

00:28:31.126 --> 00:28:34.129
<i>these corals grow extremely slowly.</i>

00:28:36.465 --> 00:28:39.760
<i>Some reefs may be 40,000 years old.</i>

00:28:41.011 --> 00:28:46.892
<i>Despite being so far beneath the surface,</i>
<i>these corals are still within our reach.</i>

00:28:53.023 --> 00:28:56.652
<i>Deep-sea fishing nets</i>
<i>dragged across the seafloor</i>

00:28:56.735 --> 00:28:59.404
<i>reduce the fragile reefs to rubble.</i>

00:29:01.698 --> 00:29:06.078
<i>Half of all deep-sea corals</i>
<i>have already been destroyed.</i>

00:29:07.329 --> 00:29:10.791
<i>It will take centuries</i>
<i>for these communities to recover.</i>

00:29:23.011 --> 00:29:25.847
<i>Beyond the boundary of any one country,</i>

00:29:26.306 --> 00:29:29.977
<i>the high seas</i>
<i>are wild and barely protected.</i>

00:29:33.897 --> 00:29:36.483
<i>Wildest of all, the Southern Ocean,</i>

00:29:38.986 --> 00:29:40.946
<i>home of the albatross.</i>

00:29:49.162 --> 00:29:53.500
<i>Albatross live solitary lives</i>
<i>far out to sea.</i>

00:29:59.339 --> 00:30:02.092
<i>They can travel</i>
<i>hundreds of kilometers a day,</i>

00:30:02.175 --> 00:30:05.762
<i>often for weeks at a time,</i>
<i>searching for food.</i>

00:30:10.058 --> 00:30:13.395
<i>A dead sea lion is a rare opportunity.</i>

00:30:24.156 --> 00:30:26.825
<i>Giant petrels are the first to arrive.</i>

00:30:29.578 --> 00:30:33.290
<i>They use their powerful beaks</i>
<i>to rip the carcass open.</i>

00:30:35.625 --> 00:30:37.085
<i>Known as sea vultures,</i>

00:30:37.169 --> 00:30:40.881
<i>they are the top scavenger</i>
<i>in Antarctic waters.</i>

00:30:45.552 --> 00:30:47.554
[cawing]

00:30:54.102 --> 00:30:58.190
<i>Black-browed albatross</i>
<i>have an extraordinary sense of smell</i>

00:30:58.273 --> 00:31:01.777
<i>and can follow a scent</i>
<i>from 20 kilometers away.</i>

00:31:06.615 --> 00:31:09.618
<i>The black-browed</i>
<i>must grab mouthfuls when they can.</i>

00:31:14.998 --> 00:31:19.461
<i>They are bullied</i>
<i>by the more aggressive giant petrels.</i>

00:31:37.187 --> 00:31:39.898
<i>Wilson's petrels are too small to compete</i>

00:31:39.981 --> 00:31:42.984
<i>and pick off scraps</i>
<i>from the edge of the frenzy.</i>

00:31:48.615 --> 00:31:53.620
<i>These delicate little petrels</i>
<i>are the most numerous of all seabirds</i>

00:31:53.703 --> 00:31:55.831
<i>because they're found in every ocean.</i>

00:31:58.625 --> 00:32:00.210
[cawing and squawking]

00:32:03.463 --> 00:32:05.966
<i>It's only when</i>
<i>these normally solitary birds</i>

00:32:06.049 --> 00:32:07.342
<i>gather like this</i>

00:32:07.426 --> 00:32:12.097
<i>that we can appreciate the wealth of life</i>
<i>supported by the high seas.</i>

00:32:25.861 --> 00:32:29.406
<i>A wandering albatross is last to arrive.</i>

00:32:37.581 --> 00:32:41.626
<i>It dwarfs the other birds</i>
<i>and uses its great size</i>

00:32:42.544 --> 00:32:45.630
<i>and three-meter wingspan to dominate.</i>

00:32:56.266 --> 00:32:58.768
<i>Even the giant petrels back off.</i>

00:33:11.865 --> 00:33:14.493
<i>The wanderer finally gets its share.</i>

00:33:26.630 --> 00:33:30.008
<i>In recent years,</i>
<i>albatross and other seabirds</i>

00:33:30.091 --> 00:33:31.676
<i>have been in decline.</i>

00:33:33.637 --> 00:33:36.973
<i>Their need to travel great distances</i>
<i>exposes them</i>

00:33:37.057 --> 00:33:41.770
<i>to the dangers of a poorly regulated</i>
<i>and overexploited ocean.</i>

00:33:43.605 --> 00:33:46.858
<i>It's a problem</i>
<i>for all hunters of the high seas.</i>

00:33:50.946 --> 00:33:55.450
<i>These giants cross entire oceans</i>
<i>in their search for food.</i>

00:33:58.828 --> 00:34:03.375
<i>They can grow over three meters long</i>
<i>and weigh half a ton.</i>

00:34:10.549 --> 00:34:11.925
<i>Bluefin tuna.</i>

00:34:14.678 --> 00:34:18.890
<i>They're streamlined to perfection</i>
<i>and built for speed.</i>

00:34:23.895 --> 00:34:27.274
<i>They hunt in great packs, hundreds strong.</i>

00:34:50.755 --> 00:34:53.717
<i>The target: a school of anchovies.</i>

00:35:08.189 --> 00:35:13.153
<i>They gently corral the anchovies</i>
<i>into a tight ball at the surface,</i>

00:35:16.489 --> 00:35:18.867
<i>careful not to cause panic.</i>

00:35:22.162 --> 00:35:23.997
<i>And then they attack.</i>

00:35:40.055 --> 00:35:42.515
<i>This is a highly coordinated hunt.</i>

00:35:42.599 --> 00:35:44.267
<i>The tuna take turns,</i>

00:35:44.351 --> 00:35:47.979
<i>striking from the same direction</i>
<i>to keep the anchovies on the run.</i>

00:35:56.613 --> 00:36:01.368
<i>After a mouthful, each bluefin peels off</i>
<i>to take its place at the rear.</i>

00:36:02.661 --> 00:36:05.705
<i>Wave after wave continues the assault.</i>

00:36:21.638 --> 00:36:23.932
<i>With their power and devastating pace,</i>

00:36:24.015 --> 00:36:28.436
<i>bluefin tuna are one of the ocean's</i>
<i>most impressive hunters.</i>

00:36:39.781 --> 00:36:43.410
<i>They are also</i>
<i>the most prized fish in the sea.</i>

00:36:46.121 --> 00:36:50.542
<i>So valuable</i>
<i>that a single bluefin can sell in Japan</i>

00:36:50.625 --> 00:36:52.669
<i>for over a million dollars.</i>

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:57.549
<i>So, perhaps it's inevitable</i>

00:36:57.632 --> 00:37:00.760
<i>that they have been fished</i>
<i>almost to extinction.</i>

00:37:05.849 --> 00:37:09.477
<i>Bluefin tuna</i>
<i>are not the only ones in peril.</i>

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:16.025
<i>Decades of unsustainable fishing</i>

00:37:16.109 --> 00:37:19.237
<i>have left many fish stocks</i>
<i>in serious decline.</i>

00:37:22.866 --> 00:37:25.076
<i>A third have collapsed altogether.</i>

00:37:29.122 --> 00:37:32.292
<i>Plastic pollution</i>
<i>is a grave issue for our oceans,</i>

00:37:33.126 --> 00:37:37.213
<i>but industrial overfishing</i>
<i>is far more dangerous.</i>

00:37:39.758 --> 00:37:43.344
<i>If we continue to harvest the seas</i>
<i>in this way,</i>

00:37:43.428 --> 00:37:46.014
<i>it's not just fisheries</i>
<i>that will collapse.</i>

00:37:48.683 --> 00:37:51.978
<i>The whole ocean system could follow.</i>

00:37:58.485 --> 00:38:02.280
<i>One hundred million sharks</i>
<i>are killed every year,</i>

00:38:04.032 --> 00:38:06.409
<i>just to make shark fin soup.</i>

00:38:16.419 --> 00:38:21.090
<i>Ninety percent of all large ocean hunters</i>
<i>have disappeared.</i>

00:38:25.845 --> 00:38:31.059
<i>Without them at the top of the food chain,</i>
<i>the whole community of marine life</i>

00:38:31.643 --> 00:38:35.271
<i>is declining</i>
<i>and changing beyond recognition.</i>

00:38:56.668 --> 00:39:00.755
<i>Squid are increasingly replacing fish.</i>

00:39:07.720 --> 00:39:12.475
<i>We have severely depleted</i>
<i>both their predators and competitors,</i>

00:39:13.643 --> 00:39:15.979
<i>so squid are taking over,</i>

00:39:19.232 --> 00:39:23.570
<i>an indication</i>
<i>of a serious imbalance in the oceans.</i>

00:39:29.701 --> 00:39:33.371
<i>Squid breed quickly</i>
<i>and have lots of fast-growing young,</i>

00:39:35.164 --> 00:39:40.086
<i>so they can rapidly exploit the gaps</i>
<i>left by the fish that we have harvested.</i>

00:39:42.213 --> 00:39:44.674
<i>They lay their eggs in cases</i>
<i>on the seafloor.</i>

00:39:44.757 --> 00:39:49.304
<i>The young develop even faster</i>
<i>in these warm, shallow waters.</i>

00:39:52.557 --> 00:39:55.727
<i>With such drastic changes</i>
<i>in the marine menu,</i>

00:39:55.810 --> 00:39:58.271
<i>predators are having to adapt.</i>

00:40:02.734 --> 00:40:06.321
<i>Sea lions prefer to eat</i>
<i>more energy-rich prey,</i>

00:40:06.404 --> 00:40:08.489
<i>like anchovies and sardines,</i>

00:40:09.282 --> 00:40:11.367
<i>but with these in short supply,</i>

00:40:11.451 --> 00:40:14.662
<i>they're forced to rely more</i>
<i>on a diet of squid.</i>

00:40:19.584 --> 00:40:23.171
<i>We, too, will be forced to change</i>
<i>the seafood we eat</i>

00:40:23.254 --> 00:40:25.673
<i>if we continue to fish as we do.</i>

00:40:29.802 --> 00:40:33.264
<i>But if we harvest the oceans</i>
<i>in a sustainable way,</i>

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:36.684
<i>they can be marvelously productive</i>

00:40:37.685 --> 00:40:40.688
<i>and will provide us</i>
<i>with an abundance of food.</i>

00:40:46.819 --> 00:40:49.572
<i>There are already signs</i>
<i>that the ocean has the power</i>

00:40:49.656 --> 00:40:52.033
<i>to recover astonishingly quickly.</i>

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:03.378
<i>Humpback whales.</i>

00:41:11.219 --> 00:41:13.262
<i>They live in every ocean,</i>

00:41:13.346 --> 00:41:16.849
<i>traveling the high seas,</i>
<i>from their polar feeding grounds</i>

00:41:16.933 --> 00:41:18.893
<i>to the tropics, where they breed.</i>

00:41:19.727 --> 00:41:21.062
[whale song]

00:41:22.897 --> 00:41:26.567
<i>Yet whales have not always enjoyed</i>
<i>such freedom.</i>

00:41:29.696 --> 00:41:33.074
<i>Once, there were</i>
<i>more than a hundred thousand humpbacks</i>

00:41:33.157 --> 00:41:34.033
<i>in the oceans,</i>

00:41:35.118 --> 00:41:39.080
<i>but last century,</i>
<i>they were hunted to near extinction.</i>

00:41:44.794 --> 00:41:48.172
<i>Just a few thousand</i>
<i>survived the onslaught.</i>

00:41:56.848 --> 00:41:58.766
<i>A huge public outcry</i>

00:41:59.183 --> 00:42:04.147
<i>finally led to a ban </i>
<i>on commercial whaling in 1986.</i>

00:42:07.734 --> 00:42:12.613
<i>Since then, the number of humpback whales</i>
<i>has been steadily increasing.</i>

00:42:18.619 --> 00:42:21.497
<i>They're returning</i>
<i>to their ancestral feeding grounds,</i>

00:42:21.581 --> 00:42:24.083
<i>like these off the coast of South Africa.</i>

00:42:26.044 --> 00:42:29.630
[whale song]

00:42:33.468 --> 00:42:38.056
<i>They are gathering to take advantage</i>
<i>of a seasonal abundance of krill,</i>

00:42:38.139 --> 00:42:40.183
<i>tiny shrimp-like crustaceans.</i>

00:42:52.779 --> 00:42:55.490
<i>The whales take in</i>
<i>great mouthfuls of water,</i>

00:42:56.240 --> 00:42:59.243
<i>trapping the krill</i>
<i>on sieves of hairy bristles</i>

00:42:59.327 --> 00:43:00.703
<i>that line their jaws.</i>

00:43:22.517 --> 00:43:25.812
<i>Each whale can eat over a ton a day.</i>

00:43:56.759 --> 00:44:01.722
<i>With food so plentiful,</i>
<i>fur seals come to claim their share.</i>

00:44:03.099 --> 00:44:07.103
[whale song]

00:44:24.829 --> 00:44:29.834
<i>These extraordinary scenes</i>
<i>were unimaginable just a few years ago.</i>

00:44:45.892 --> 00:44:49.312
<i>They form super-groups, hundreds strong.</i>

00:45:11.709 --> 00:45:17.006
<i>This is the greatest gathering of whales</i>
<i>seen for a century.</i>

00:45:37.193 --> 00:45:41.572
<i>Whales recycle nutrients</i>
<i>that enrich surface waters,</i>

00:45:42.114 --> 00:45:44.700
<i>which fuel the growth of phytoplankton,</i>

00:45:45.618 --> 00:45:50.581
<i>and they, in turn, feed krill</i>
<i>in a perfect self-sustaining cycle.</i>

00:45:53.542 --> 00:45:57.046
<i>We now know that a healthy community</i>
<i>of great hunters,</i>

00:45:57.129 --> 00:46:00.174
<i>whales, dolphins, tuna, and sharks,</i>

00:46:01.092 --> 00:46:04.095
<i>is essential</i>
<i>for a fully-functioning ocean.</i>

00:46:07.431 --> 00:46:11.894
<i>And a functioning ocean</i>
<i>is vital to the health of our planet</i>

00:46:12.520 --> 00:46:13.771
<i>and humanity.</i>

00:46:18.276 --> 00:46:20.528
<i>In a remarkable recovery,</i>

00:46:20.611 --> 00:46:24.448
<i>humpback whales have almost returned</i>
<i>to their original numbers.</i>

00:46:27.493 --> 00:46:29.495
<i>But during that time,</i>

00:46:29.578 --> 00:46:34.917
<i>we have done more harm to the oceans</i>
<i>than ever before in human history.</i>

00:46:44.302 --> 00:46:47.346
<i>Only with global cooperation</i>

00:46:47.430 --> 00:46:51.183
<i>will our oceans</i>
<i>recover and thrive once again.</i>

00:46:55.146 --> 00:46:58.607
<i>We saved the whales</i>
<i>by international agreement.</i>

00:47:01.027 --> 00:47:05.072
<i>Now, it is time to save our oceans.</i>

00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:22.798
<i>Please visit ourplanet.com</i>
<i>to discover what we need to do now</i>

00:47:22.882 --> 00:47:24.884
<i>to protect the high seas.</i>

00:47:27.345 --> 00:47:33.184
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Singing together ♪</i>

00:47:35.895 --> 00:47:41.776
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say it's now or never ♪</i>

00:47:44.612 --> 00:47:48.908
<i>♪ 'Cause it's not too late</i>
<i>If we change our ways ♪</i>

00:47:48.991 --> 00:47:52.787
<i>♪ And connect the dots to our problems ♪</i>

00:47:52.870 --> 00:47:58.918
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say we're in this together ♪</i>

00:47:59.001 --> 00:48:00.795
<i>♪ We're in this together ♪</i>

00:48:01.379 --> 00:48:03.381
[vocalizing]